        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A cordless drill battery typically lasts a few hundred full cycles, about two to five years for most owners and longer for light users who store packs well. Runtime per charge comes down to watt-hours and the job: a 2.0Ah pack handles light household work for an afternoon, while heavy drilling calls for a 4.0Ah or larger pack. Heat, cold charging and storage at full or empty charge are what wear packs out early.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Keep batteries cool, dry and roughly half charged for long breaks, use the matching charger and retire any pack that swells, cracks or overheats. When a replacement is due, compare the price of a single pack with a kit deal before you buy. Our cordless drill rankings cover the best current kits and the batteries they include.
          </p>
